{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T05:29:39.823Z","ordinal":518593,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18b47-a54b-7cb0-a56a-8243423701cb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-918f06e5-785c-43d7-8e76-9991fbe49d3b","process_id":"57778","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '50,105p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/README.md && sed -n '55,85p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/README.md && sed -n '58,72p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/SHA256SUMS && rg -n '65 entries|67 entries|entries verify|manifest' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/pr334-evidence-correction","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '50,105p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/README.md","name":"README.md","path":"docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/README.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '55,85p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/README.md","name":"README.md","path":"docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/README.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '58,72p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/SHA256SUMS","name":"SHA256SUMS","path":"docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/SHA256SUMS"},{"type":"search","cmd":"rg -n '65 entries|67 entries|entries verify|manifest' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2","query":"65 entries|67 entries|entries verify|manifest","path":"platform-e2"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"|---|---|---|\n| Checksum tamper | Installer refused with `SHA-256 verification failed; no installation was changed.`; launcher symlink and synthetic state marker unchanged | [log](ubuntu-22-04-node22-checksum-tamper.log) |\n| Broken candidate self-test | `nexus upgrade --version v1.0.0-rc.10 --force --yes` against a fixture whose launcher reports `9.9.9` failed with `Unpacked binary failed staging self-test`; existing install and user state unchanged | [log](ubuntu-22-04-node22-upgrade-self-test-failure.log) |\n| Backup restore | Recognized private `~/.nexus/backups/...` backup restored state and config with 0700 directories / 0600 files | [log](ubuntu-22-04-node22-backup-restore.log) |\n| Updater process crash | Updater SIGKILLed after the durable journal reached `switched`; `nexus upgrade --recover` restored the verified RC10 package, retained the failed target for diagnosis, asserted that the journal's staging directory and any `.tmp-upgrade-*` entry were removed, and was idempotent. **Power loss is not tested** | [log](ubuntu-22-04-node22-upgrade-process-crash-recovery.log) |\n| Incompatible downgrade | A synthetic release (`prepare-incompatible-fixture.sh`) declares state schema 1; `nexus upgrade --version v1.0.0-rc.4 --allow-downgrade --yes` was refused with `uses state schema version 1, which cannot read current state schema version 2`. Installed version, launcher target and package hash were unchanged and no target directory was created | [log](ubuntu-22-04-node22-incompatible-downgrade.log) |\n| Synthetic journal recovery | `run-synthetic-journal-recovery.sh` writes a valid `staged` journal with a staging directory, then a `preparing` journal without one: `nexus upgrade --recover` reports `cleaned_up`, removes staging, keeps the launcher and version, and creates no target directory; a malformed journal is refused with `failed schema or path validation`; the script verifies the installed version string and launcher symlink target are unchanged and that the target install directory remains absent (it does not compare package contents or user state); a second recovery is a no-op | [log](ubuntu-22-04-node22-synthetic-journal-recovery.log) |\n| Power-loss durability | **FAIL on the published RC10 artifact.** Power was cut (QEMU SIGKILL, ext4, `cache=none`) at the durable `switched` journal state. After reboot `v1.0.0-rc.10/bin/nexus` is 0 bytes, `nexus --version` prints nothing, and `nexus upgrade --recover` does nothing and exits 0 (a 0-byte launcher is an empty shell script), so the installation cannot repair itself. Cause: the extracted package was never fsynced before the rename and launcher switch. With a CLI containing the fix (PR #333) the same cut leaves a working launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, staging is removed, the stale lock is reclaimed, a second recovery is a no-op, and a retry completes to rc.10 | [RC10](ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-verify.log) · [fixed CLI](ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-fixed-cli-verify.log) |\n| Power-loss durability (RC11) | **FAIL on the published RC11 artifact** (tag `8d4e5319`, which predates the fix). The same cut during rc.10 → rc.11 leaves `v1.0.0-rc.11/bin/nexus` at 0 bytes, `nexus --version` empty, and `nexus upgrade --recover` exiting 0 without doing anything. Any release built before #333 carries this defect | [log](ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-rc11-verify.log) |\n| Provenance attestation | `gh attestation verify nexus-linux-x86_64-v1.0.0-rc.10.tar.gz -R mikeholownych/nexus-code` exited 0 on the published archive (SHA-256 `444d0812…b3e7`) with a Sigstore bundle; `--format json` returned a 13,989-byte verification result | [log](attestation-rc10.log) |\n\nThe other additional lifecycle scripts do not set `NEXUS_VERIFY_ATTESTATION`; their installer calls use the installer's default `auto` mode. The incompatible-downgrade and synthetic-journal-recovery scripts set it to `0` explicitly. Provenance is verified separately by the attestation cell. Provenance attestation is verified independently by the attestation cell above.\n\nPer-cell logs and results: `results.tsv`; image names and IDs: `prepared-images.json`; digests: `SHA256SUMS`.\n\n## Incompatible-downgrade fixture\n\n`lifecycle/prepare-incompatible-fixture.sh <rc10-archive> <out>` builds a synthetic `v1.0.0-rc.4` release from the published RC10 package (same contents, `compatibility.json` rewritten to declare state schema 1 and the rc.4 archive binding) and writes a local API JSON. `lifecycle/run-incompatible-downgrade.sh` installs RC10 in a container, serves the fixture locally, and asserts the refusal plus an unchanged installation. No published RC declares an older state schema (rc.5–rc.10 all declare 2), which is why the fixture is synthetic.\n\n## Power-loss durability\n\n`lifecycle/powerloss/` builds a 2 GiB ext4 image with rc.9 installed, boots a minimal QEMU/KVM guest, starts the upgrade to rc.10, and `SIGKILL`s the VM when the durable journal reaches `switched` — package in place and launcher switched, runtime verification not yet run. Nothing is synced first, and the image is mounted `cache=none`, so dirty page cache is lost exactly as in a power cut. The second boot dumps the journal, upgrade log and package directories, parses every JSON state file, runs `nexus upgrade --recover`, and asserts the rollback, staging removal, stale-lock reclamation and idempotence, then retries the upgrade to completion.\n\nOn the published RC10 artifact the cell **fails**: the extracted package's file data was never fsynced, so the rename and launcher switch survived while the payload did not. `v1.0.0-rc.10/bin/nexus` is 0 bytes, `nexus --version` prints nothing, and `nexus upgrade --recover` exits 0 without doing anything, because a 0-byte launcher is an empty shell script. The upgrade is performed by the installed rc.9 CLI, and the missing flush is in that running CLI. The published RC11 artifact (tag `8d4e5319`) reproduces the same failure for rc.10 → rc.11, because the tag predates the fix.\n\nThe fix (PR #333) flushes every extracted file and directory before the switch. `NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` runs the same published artifacts through a CLI containing the fix (the guest records the SHA-256 of the dist's sorted file manifest); that cut leaves a working launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, and the retry completes to rc.10. This demonstrates the fix; it is not qualification. The cell can only pass for a release whose own artifact contains the fix.\n\nThe harness needs KVM. Under TCG emulation the CLI's 5 s package-health probe can exceed its budget on a cold start and produce a false failure unrelated to durability.\n\n## Synthetic journal states\n\n`lifecycle/run-synthetic-journal-recovery.sh` writes journals conforming to the real schema (fixed source/target paths, staging name and timestamps) for the `staged` and `preparing` states, plus a malformed file, so recovery can be exercised without killing a live updater. It asserts the `cleaned_up` action, staging removal, launcher/version stability, refusal of the malformed journal, and idempotence.\n\n## Reproduce\n\n```sh\ncp install.sh /tmp/nexus-install.sh   # release asset v1.0.0-rc.10\nbash docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/run-rc10-e2-matrix.sh\n```\n\nThe positive cells use Docker `--network bridge` to download the published RC10 release assets; the negative cells run `--network none`. The script refuses to start unless the installer hash matches the one recorded here.\n\n## Scope and residuals\n\n- This covers the fresh-install / doctor / Node-preflight matrix plus the lifecycle cells (Ubuntu 22.04, Debian 12, Fedora 40 / Node 22) for E2 (ACC-PLAT) on RC10: install, installer-driven version switch and rollback, reinstall, repair and uninstall; checksum tamper; broken-candidate self-test; backup restore; updater process-crash recovery with staging cleanup; incompatible downgrade from a synthetic state-schema-1 release; synthetic-journal recovery from early transaction states and a malformed journal; and provenance-attestation verification. An ordinary successful `nexus upgrade` from alpha.2 is not executable because alpha.2 has no updater command; the alpha.2 row is installer-driven. Power-loss durability is now tested rather than assumed, and it **fails on both RC10 and RC11** (RC11 predates the fix): see the rows and section below.\n- `endpoint.reachable` failing is expected in the isolated containers and is not a product failure; the same doctor run on a host with a configured gateway is the installed-RC provider evidence path.\n- Images were prepared for the RC9 matrix (`prep-182e8addeee0`). `prepared-images.json` records each cell label, image reference and image ID; it does not store separate base-distribution or Node-version fields. Positive-cell logs print the container's `node --version`; Node 24 cells mount the host Node v24.11.1 runtime into the Node 22 image.\n## Result on the published RC10 artifact: FAIL\n\n`v1.0.0-rc.10/bin/nexus` is **0 bytes** after the cut (the verify log prints the\nsize). The package was renamed into place and the launcher switched, but its\nfile data was never fsynced, so the power loss left the launcher pointing at\nempty files. `nexus --version` prints nothing, and `nexus upgrade --recover`\n**exits 0 without doing anything** — a 0-byte launcher is an empty shell script,\nso the installation cannot repair itself and an automation check on the exit\nstatus would read the failed recovery as success.\n\nThe fix is in `cli/src/durable_tree.ts` (PR #333): `safeExtractTar` flushes every\nfile and directory of the extracted tree before the caller renames it and\nswitches the launcher.\n\n## Result on the published RC11 artifact: FAIL\n\n`v1.0.0-rc.11` (tag `8d4e5319`) predates the fix, so rc.10 → rc.11 reproduces the\nsame failure: `v1.0.0-rc.11/bin/nexus` is 0 bytes, `nexus --version` is empty, and\n`nexus upgrade --recover` exits 0 without doing anything\n(`...-power-loss-durability-rc11-verify.log`). Any release built before #333\ncarries the defect.\n\n## Fix demonstration\n\n`NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` replaces the baseline CLI's `lib/cli/dist` with a\nlocal build, so the same published artifacts are upgraded by a CLI that contains\nthe fix. `NEXUS_POWERLOSS_LOG_SUFFIX=fixed-cli` names the resulting logs, and the\nguest records the SHA-256 of the dist's sorted file manifest in them (hashing only\n`index.js` would not cover `durable_tree.js`, which carries the fix). With it, the identical cut leaves a\nworking launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, and a retry completes to rc.10\n(`...-power-loss-durability-fixed-cli-{write,verify}.log`).\n7a6094fd01a5cd392d3488eb790132d97709071415d7b6e3e14110ed12330c0f  lifecycle/powerloss/fetch-assets.sh\n842fce3418bef41be4adb2ca5f15fea0c6e8c96496a58c2e45df4e121897172a  lifecycle/powerloss/guest-init\n18f19fa27e480e299661faa3b89c7fcbe685452ad6248f62b656ef8554d6a682  lifecycle/powerloss/run-power-loss-durability.sh\na42926f99a61874b2d42107875dbd4497310cfdcd5295b4a7762b59f49f5c6a5  lifecycle/powerloss/serve-release.cjs\naa645ed28ed24c40a0274521c3b8c38330c007ba99a564ce5df9f1851c79d3d6  ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-write.log\n0501008ea1588bab704b741f88eb53722328ef70619323e8c462970bf677e0c2  ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-verify.log\n7d96e2ebcc55b758bd2ac6047e2a770a28130d30b7b08250875b186f5c19caa0  ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-fixed-cli-write.log\n8fa6c9ee6392434e4c90cecbcbe7b07775cc15b9dae07c7f01dccacd20e6a142  ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-fixed-cli-verify.log\nb0afaa282f96f2c8d78421ed393cc8f7024a5f3d61989904473f409b481ddb21  ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-rc11-write.log\n69a96131328e9fb0c20761d356bdc847496b758b7c881f130405a6dae0f54786  ubuntu-22-04-node22-power-loss-durability-rc11-verify.log\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/README.md:75:The fix (PR #333) flushes every extracted file and directory before the switch. `NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` runs the same published artifacts through a CLI containing the fix (the guest records the SHA-256 of the dist's sorted file manifest); that cut leaves a working launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, and the retry completes to rc.10. This demonstrates the fix; it is not qualification. The cell can only pass for a release whose own artifact contains the fix.\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/prepare-lifecycle-fixtures.sh:29:(cd \"$out/broken-release/pkg/nexus\" && find . -type f ! -name MANIFEST.sha256 -print0 | sort -z | xargs -0 sha256sum > MANIFEST.sha256)\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/prepare-lifecycle-fixtures.sh:41:(cd \"$out/process-crash-release/pkg/nexus\" && find . -type f ! -name MANIFEST.sha256 -print0 | sort -z | xargs -0 sha256sum > MANIFEST.sha256)\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/prepare-incompatible-fixture.sh:19:(cd \"$out/release/pkg/nexus\" && find . -type f ! -name MANIFEST.sha256 -print0 | sort -z | xargs -0 sha256sum > MANIFEST.sha256)\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/README.md:82:guest records the SHA-256 of the dist's sorted file manifest in them (hashing only\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/guest-init:25:  echo \"BASELINE_CLI_DIST=patched dist_manifest_sha256=$fixedcli (fix demonstration, not release qualification)\"\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/run-power-loss-durability.sh:96:  # Hash the whole dist (sorted file manifest), not just the entry point: the fix\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":946989315}},"started_at_ms":1793338178875,"completed_at_ms":1793338179823}}
