{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T05:29:55.353Z","ordinal":518609,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18b47-a54b-7cb0-a56a-8243423701cb","session_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","root_turn_id":"01a18b47-a54b-7cb0-a56a-8243423701cb","response_id":"resp_0db389702a7164cc016ae42b513a78819fb4f6e0161ec22ccd","usage":{"input_tokens":173765,"cached_input_tokens":171392,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":196,"reasoning_output_tokens":75,"total_tokens":173961},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":2115177,"cached_input_tokens":2089984,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":8310,"reasoning_output_tokens":5006,"total_tokens":2123487},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":9754689776,"cached_input_tokens":9651194752,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":28066423,"reasoning_output_tokens":16509215,"total_tokens":9782756199}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T05:29:57.951Z","ordinal":518610,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18b47-a54b-7cb0-a56a-8243423701cb","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-ce1fe8d7-93e6-4e1a-bfb5-b3879b011347","process_id":"58405","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '70,78p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/README.md && sed -n '68,79p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/README.md"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/pr334-evidence-correction","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '70,78p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/README.md","name":"README.md","path":"docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/README.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '68,79p' docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/README.md","name":"README.md","path":"docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc.10/platform-e2/lifecycle/powerloss/README.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"\n`lifecycle/powerloss/` builds a 2 GiB ext4 image with rc.9 installed, boots a minimal QEMU/KVM guest, starts the upgrade to rc.10, and `SIGKILL`s the VM when the durable journal reaches `switched` — package in place and launcher switched, runtime verification not yet run. Nothing is synced first, and the image is mounted `cache=none`, so dirty page cache is lost exactly as in a power cut. The second boot dumps the journal, upgrade log and package directories, parses every JSON state file, runs `nexus upgrade --recover`, and asserts the rollback, staging removal, stale-lock reclamation and idempotence, then retries the upgrade to completion.\n\nOn the published RC10 artifact the cell **fails**: the extracted package's file data was never fsynced, so the rename and launcher switch survived while the payload did not. `v1.0.0-rc.10/bin/nexus` is 0 bytes, `nexus --version` prints nothing, and `nexus upgrade --recover` exits 0 without doing anything, because a 0-byte launcher is an empty shell script. The upgrade is performed by the installed rc.9 CLI, and the missing flush is in that running CLI. The published RC11 artifact (tag `8d4e5319`) reproduces the same failure for rc.10 → rc.11, because the tag predates the fix.\n\nThe fix (PR #333) flushes every extracted file and directory before the switch. `NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` runs the same published artifacts through a CLI containing the fix (the guest records the SHA-256 of the dist's sorted file manifest); that cut leaves a working launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, and the retry completes to rc.10. This demonstrates the fix; it is not qualification. The cell can only pass for a release whose own artifact contains the fix.\n\nThe harness needs KVM. Under TCG emulation the CLI's 5 s package-health probe can exceed its budget on a cold start and produce a false failure unrelated to durability.\n\n\n## Result on the published RC11 artifact: FAIL\n\n`v1.0.0-rc.11` (tag `8d4e5319`) predates the fix, so rc.10 → rc.11 reproduces the\nsame failure: `v1.0.0-rc.11/bin/nexus` is 0 bytes, `nexus --version` is empty, and\n`nexus upgrade --recover` exits 0 without doing anything\n(`...-power-loss-durability-rc11-verify.log`). Any release built before #333\ncarries the defect.\n\n## Fix demonstration\n\n`NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` replaces the baseline CLI's `lib/cli/dist` with a\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":2,"nanos":100278812}},"started_at_ms":1793338195849,"completed_at_ms":1793338197951}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T05:29:58.254Z","ordinal":518611,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a18b49-48ee-7250-b6c4-a371f9e8a12f","call_id":"call_c125f59313ff462b9506723b54bdbd61","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 2.9 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"\n`lifecycle/powerloss/` builds a 2 GiB ext4 image with rc.9 installed, boots a minimal QEMU/KVM guest, starts the upgrade to rc.10, and `SIGKILL`s the VM when the durable journal reaches `switched` — package in place and launcher switched, runtime verification not yet run. Nothing is synced first, and the image is mounted `cache=none`, so dirty page cache is lost exactly as in a power cut. The second boot dumps the journal, upgrade log and package directories, parses every JSON state file, runs `nexus upgrade --recover`, and asserts the rollback, staging removal, stale-lock reclamation and idempotence, then retries the upgrade to completion.\n\nOn the published RC10 artifact the cell **fails**: the extracted package's file data was never fsynced, so the rename and launcher switch survived while the payload did not. `v1.0.0-rc.10/bin/nexus` is 0 bytes, `nexus --version` prints nothing, and `nexus upgrade --recover` exits 0 without doing anything, because a 0-byte launcher is an empty shell script. The upgrade is performed by the installed rc.9 CLI, and the missing flush is in that running CLI. The published RC11 artifact (tag `8d4e5319`) reproduces the same failure for rc.10 → rc.11, because the tag predates the fix.\n\nThe fix (PR #333) flushes every extracted file and directory before the switch. `NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` runs the same published artifacts through a CLI containing the fix (the guest records the SHA-256 of the dist's sorted file manifest); that cut leaves a working launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, and the retry completes to rc.10. This demonstrates the fix; it is not qualification. The cell can only pass for a release whose own artifact contains the fix.\n\nThe harness needs KVM. Under TCG emulation the CLI's 5 s package-health probe can exceed its budget on a cold start and produce a false failure unrelated to durability.\n\n\n## Result on the published RC11 artifact: FAIL\n\n`v1.0.0-rc.11` (tag `8d4e5319`) predates the fix, so rc.10 → rc.11 reproduces the\nsame failure: `v1.0.0-rc.11/bin/nexus` is 0 bytes, `nexus --version` is empty, and\n`nexus upgrade --recover` exits 0 without doing anything\n(`...-power-loss-durability-rc11-verify.log`). Any release built before #333\ncarries the defect.\n\n## Fix demonstration\n\n`NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` replaces the baseline CLI's `lib/cli/dist` with a\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18b47-a54b-7cb0-a56a-8243423701cb","create_time":1793338198.2547343}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
