{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T05:42:28.891Z","ordinal":519441,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18b4e-a8d9-78f0-bd60-966a1e9e4357","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-c6219fbc-817d-46bf-a7fa-0abbc5f1c642","process_id":"41345","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,220p' docs/releases/v1.0.0.md 2>/dev/null | tail -100; find docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification -maxdepth 2 -type f | sort | tail -35"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-wt/rc13-source-gates-20261030T0537Z","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"sed -n '1,220p' docs/releases/v1.0.0.md 2>/dev/null | tail -100; find docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification -maxdepth 2 -type f | sort | tail -35"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"- Plugins execute in-process with full user authority. The legacy worker-thread sandbox claim has been removed, and `executeSandboxed` deleted.\n- The undocumented foreign plugin directory `~/.config/opencode/plugins` has been removed. Plugins are only loaded from `~/.config/nexus/plugins/`, `~/.nexus/plugins/`, and trusted workspace directories.\n- Any plugin middleware rewrite of tool arguments or results is cryptographically recorded with before and after SHA-256 digests in both `~/.nexus/audit.log` and the session flight log (EXT-004).\n\n### Protected Path Hardening (REQ-SEC-118)\n- File protection rules now match `.env*` prefixes (e.g. `.env`, `.env.local`, `.env.production`), `id_rsa*`, and `id_ed25519*` case-insensitively across path components and canonical targets resolving symlinks.\n- Protection is enforced across all file read, write, and edit tools (`read_file`, `batch_read`, `edit_file`, `patch_file`, `write_file`, etc.). Path protection remains advisory for `run_command`.\n\n### Web Search Honesty (REQ-PRV-108)\n- `search_web` returns `success: false` and empty results when no search hits are found (no fabricated results). Its search destination is disclosed on first use and gated by egress disclosure.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Safe Execution Defaults & Approval Integrity (PLAN-01, DEC-01 Option C)\n\n### Approval Mode Independent of Folder Trust (amended DEC-01)\n- **Problem addressed (SEC-01):** Folder trust and tool authority were coupled, making workspace trust silently change the approval posture.\n- **New behavior:** Tool permission mode is independent of workspace trust and defaults to `review` in every folder. `--mode auto` selects autonomous writes and command execution while keeping first-use egress approval per origin. `--yes` and `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto` select full access and bypass all approval prompts, including egress.\n  - **Scripts and CI:** Automated scripts, test runners, and CI environments that must not prompt should pass `--yes` or set `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto`.\n- **Trusting your own repository:** Run `nexus trust .` (or `nexus trust <path>`) to load project instructions and workspace integrations. Trust does not change tool permission mode.\n- **Posture flags:**\n  - `--approve` / `--mode review`: Enforces review posture regardless of workspace trust.\n  - `--mode auto`: Allows writes and commands without approval; prompts before first egress to each origin.\n  - `--yes`: Enables full access, including egress without approval.\n  - `--read-only` / `--mode read-only`: Enforces read-only posture (no file writes or mutating commands).\n\n### Startup Posture Banner (REQ-TUI-102)\n- At session start, both the interactive REPL and the Rust TUI display an explicit posture banner:\n  ```\n  mode: review · sandbox: off · workspace: untrusted (nexus trust .)\n  ```\n- `nexus doctor` outputs the active posture tuple (`mode`, `sandbox`, `trusted`).\n\n### REPL Approval Hardening (REQ-SEC-103)\n- Approvals in `InteractiveRepl` use a hardened `[y/N/d/a]` prompt.\n- **Lone Enter defaults to No:** Pressing Enter with an empty buffer safely rejects the proposed action (`N`).\n- **Input grace period & typing protection:** Input buffered within the first 400 ms of prompt display is discarded to protect against typed-ahead steering input or pastes answering the prompt by accident.\n- **Approve-all confirmation:** Selecting `a` (approve all later actions) prompts for explicit confirmation: `Type 'all' to confirm approving all later actions for this session:`.\n\n### TUI Approval Panel Alignment & Narrow Terminal Polish (TUI-09, TUI-10)\n- **Spatial arrow key alignment (TUI-09):** Approval panel buttons are laid out horizontally: `Approve` (left) · `All later` (center) · `Deny` (right). Left arrow (`←`) focuses `Approve` on the left, and Right arrow (`→`) focuses `Deny` on the right.\n- **Narrow terminal compact hints (TUI-10):** Under 60 columns (e.g. 40×14 terminal), approval panel instructions switch to compact short-form hints (e.g. `←→ choose, enter · esc denies`), ensuring all directions fit without line wrapping or truncation.\n\n### Read-Only HOME Masking & Path Confinement (REQ-SEC-104)\n- When running in `--read-only` mode with `bwrap` container sandboxing, `$HOME` is mounted with `--tmpfs $HOME`, completely masking `~/.ssh`, shell profiles, and user dotfiles from `run_command`.\n- Without `bwrap`, read-only command execution enforces path confinement: commands referencing `~`, `$HOME`, environment variable expansions (`$`), absolute paths outside the workspace, or escaping `..` sequences are refused.\n\n### Direct Argv Sandbox Spawning (REQ-SEC-109)\n- Command sandboxing builds arguments directly as an array (`argv`) and spawns the container sandbox process without passing through a host shell (`sh -c` / `bash -c`). This eliminates shell metacharacter quoting vulnerabilities on workspaces with complex paths (e.g. spaces, single quotes).\n\n### Destructive Command Tripwire Tokenization (REQ-SEC-108)\n- The destructive-command denylist is parsed with POSIX shell-words tokenization in both TypeScript and Rust, eliminating false positives (`rm -rf ./build`, `rm -rf /tmp/x`, `grep reboot`, `echo shutdown`, `chown -R me /srv`).\n- **Tripwire notice:** The command denylist is a **convenience tripwire** to catch obvious mistakes, **not a security boundary**. Known bypasses (such as `find / -delete`, `/bin/rm -rf /`, `rm -r -f /`, or Python rmtree calls) are documented non-goals that pass through the tripwire; the sandbox (`--sandbox`) and approval posture (`review`) form the security boundary.\n\n### Verifier Execution Gating (REQ-SEC-119)\n- Automated verifier executions (`run_tests`, `/test`, completion oracles) are classified as `exec` capabilities.\n- In review mode, the first verifier execution is gated by an approval prompt displaying the exact command and the resolved test script body (extracted from `package.json` `scripts.test`, Makefile recipes, or `Cargo.toml`). Folder trust does not authorize verifier execution.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Packaged Product Parity & Unified Launcher (PLAN-06)\n\n### Unified Launcher Grammar (REQ-REL-101, DEC-02, DEC-14)\n- **TTY default:** Launching `nexus` with no arguments in a terminal opens the live Ratatui TUI (`nexus-tui`), connecting via a local JSON-RPC protocol to the sidecar agent.\n- **Headless & non-TTY fallback:** When run in scripts, pipes, or without a TTY (e.g. `</dev/null`), `nexus` opens the command-line REPL.\n- **Explicit REPL:** `nexus repl` (or `nexus --repl`) explicitly launches the REPL with full option forwarding (`-m`, `-r`, `--approve`, `--yes`).\n- **Checkout parity:** `harness.sh` is simplified to execute the Node CLI directly with `NEXUS_HOME` unset, ensuring identical grammar between development checkouts and binary release packages.\n\n### Explicit `run` Subcommand for Free Text (REQ-REL-104, DEC-14)\n- Non-interactive tasks from the terminal now require the explicit `run` subcommand:\n  ```bash\n  nexus run \"Explain the system architecture\" --read-only\n  ```\n- **Error on unknown commands:** Unknown subcommands (such as `nexus notacmd`) are rejected immediately with an unknown command error instead of being silently interpreted as free-text agent prompts.\n\n### Single-Source Versioning & Core Handshake (REQ-REL-103)\n- Version numbers are synchronized from root `package.json` across CLI manifests, `core/Cargo.toml`, and the binary build identities via `scripts/sync-version.mjs`.\n- The core RPC protocol includes an initial `hello` handshake validating matching versions and protocol compatibility before execution begins.\n- CI release workflows assert version consistency across all artifacts.\n\n### Diagnostic Hardening & Packaged Remedies (REQ-DX-101 to REQ-DX-104)\n- `nexus doctor` runs with clean stderr and no unhandled event listener warnings.\n- Diagnostic remedies in packaged installs guide operators to reinstall or update user paths (`~/.local/bin`), never referencing developer build tools like `make` or `harness.sh`.\n- Sensitive data redaction preserves full filesystem paths, exempting absolute paths while sanitizing credential patterns.\n- `nexus doctor` checks `~/.config/nexus/config.env` permissions, warning if group- or world-readable and offering an automated `0600` chmod repair.\n- `tui.launch` verifies real launchability by probing `nexus-tui --live --check`.\n\n### Hardened Installer (REQ-REL-105)\n- Strict SemVer tag regex validation runs before any network requests.\n- When no stable release exists, the installer queries releases and offers an actionable prerelease hint (`no stable release yet; latest prerelease is vX — rerun with NEXUS_VERSION=vX`).\n- Reinstallation validates the installed tree against `MANIFEST.sha256` and automatically repairs damaged installations, or forces re-extraction with `NEXUS_FORCE=1`.\n\n---\n\n## Release qualification and waivers\n\n- **Manual real-terminal matrix: waived for v1.0.0 by DEC-20.** No manual five-terminal by three-size runs or per-cell evidence are claimed. The automated CI checks (§5.1) and scripted installed-RC PTY scenarios (§5.2) remain mandatory release gates and must pass.\n- **Screenshot artifacts: waived** for v1.0.0 by [DEC-18](../v1.0.0/DECISIONS.md#dec-18-v100-terminal-screenshot-artifact-waiver).\n\nThe manual-matrix waiver does not affect any other acceptance gate. Installed-RC platform and lifecycle evidence, provider and standalone qualification, held-out task quality, automated and scripted PTY gates, independent exact-RC acceptance and the requirements ledger remain governed by the release decision record.\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/resource-limits-20261021T0500Z-synthetic.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/resource-limits-20261021T0500Z-synthetic.md\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/t2-reference-calibration-2026-10-18.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261018T1311Z.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261020T0338Z-synthetic.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261020T0338Z-synthetic.md\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261020T1457Z-setup-failure.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261020T1457Z-setup-failure.md\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261020T1457Z-synthetic.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261020T1457Z-synthetic.md\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261021T0443Z-synthetic.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-20261021T0443Z-synthetic.md\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-exact-head.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/host/validate-main-8935918-synthetic.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/rc10-chatgpt-verification-20261027T174805Z.json\ndocs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/sec101-119-pty-20261025.md\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":897534057}},"started_at_ms":1793338947992,"completed_at_ms":1793338948890}}
