{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T09:45:10.540Z","ordinal":526919,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0db389702a7164cc016ae46724c4e0819f8b8c74a2707e67fe","status":"completed","call_id":"call_df38d73d74f8422f9b0b754630f82b34","name":"exec","input":"const rs = await Promise.allSettled([\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"export ENTIRE_TOKEN_STORE=file; entire brain brief \\\"Nexus v1 RC14 acceptance steps and held-out custody status\\\" --json\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":1000,\"max_output_tokens\":1200}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | sed -n '1,210p'; git show 16eeb6ee:docs/releases/v1.0.0-rc.14.md | sed -n '1,180p'\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":4200}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"gh pr view 334 --json number,title,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus,statusCheckRollup,url,body | jq '{number,title,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus,statusCheckRollup:[.statusCheckRollup[]|{name,conclusion,status}],url,body:(.body|.[0:1800])}'\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":1200})\n]); for (const r of rs) text(r.status===\"fulfilled\"?JSON.stringify(r.value):String(r.reason));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18c20-aec5-7552-93bd-755d714c245e","create_time":1793353508.7706325}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T09:45:10.630Z","ordinal":526920,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18c20-aec5-7552-93bd-755d714c245e","session_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","root_turn_id":"01a18c20-aec5-7552-93bd-755d714c245e","response_id":"resp_0db389702a7164cc016ae467227c50819f859601db1696b368","usage":{"input_tokens":81463,"cached_input_tokens":79232,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":541,"reasoning_output_tokens":214,"total_tokens":82004},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":14514832,"cached_input_tokens":14442368,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":28185,"reasoning_output_tokens":13746,"total_tokens":14543017},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":9943326840,"cached_input_tokens":9838459008,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":28491804,"reasoning_output_tokens":16749986,"total_tokens":9971818644}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T09:45:10.989Z","ordinal":526921,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18c20-aec5-7552-93bd-755d714c245e","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-6b1faf59-2baf-4bd3-bd76-708998f08552","process_id":"20469","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | sed -n '1,210p'; git show 16eeb6ee:docs/releases/v1.0.0-rc.14.md | sed -n '1,180p'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | sed -n '1,210p'; git show 16eeb6ee:docs/releases/v1.0.0-rc.14.md | sed -n '1,180p'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Nexus Code v1.0.0: release qualification procedure\n\nThis is the procedure that decides whether a release candidate (RC) may become v1.0.0. It defines:\n\n- the environments;\n- the workflow matrix;\n- the evidence each step must produce;\n- explicit pass/fail gates.\n\nThree separate questions are judged and recorded separately:\n\n| Question | Who answers it |\n|---|---|\n| **Implementation self-assessment** | Did the implementer's tests pass? |\n| **Product qualification** | Did the RC meet these gates on real workflows? |\n| **Independent acceptance** | Did a reviewer other than the implementer agree? |\n\nA gate passes only on archived evidence bound to the source commit or installed RC artifact that the gate evaluates. Source-level evidence names the exact source commit; installed-RC evidence names both that commit and the installed package digest. A green test suite alone never qualifies the product.\n\n## 1.1 Release-candidate lifecycle and evidence identity\n\nThe release sequence has five distinct stages. Passing an earlier stage is a prerequisite for the next stage, not a substitute for it. Every G0–G4 acceptance ID remains mandatory; the stage determines which exact input and evidence it evaluates, not whether the check applies.\n\n1. **Pre-RC source gates.** Before creating an RC tag, run required source CI and every G0–G4 check whose acceptance method evaluates source, a source-built test binary, or an environment without requiring a published RC. Record the full commit ID and check results. Source checks do not establish behavior of the installed package.\n2. **RC artifact construction.** After the required owner authorization, create the version-matched RC tag on that exact source commit and use the repository release workflow to build and publish the RC artifacts. Record the workflow run, tag commit, artifact digests, and provenance. The workflow-produced artifacts are the candidates for qualification; a local build or a different commit is not interchangeable. RC artifact publication is not product qualification or final release acceptance.\n3. **RC artifact gates.** Before installed-RC product qualification, run every G0–G4 check whose acceptance method requires the RC archive, RC attestation/release, or workflow-produced artifact. This includes ACC-SUP-01/02/03/06/08. ACC-PKG checks that explicitly require an installed package run against this RC as specified by their test method. Record the RC tag commit, workflow run, artifact digests, and each check result. A source-level analogue does not replace an artifact-bound check.\n4. **Installed-RC qualification.** Install the workflow-produced RC package using the documented procedure and run the applicable product, provider, task-quality, terminal, installation, and independent-acceptance gates in §§4–8. Bind each result to both the RC source commit and installed package digest. If a source change or rebuilt artifact changes either identity, rerun every affected gate against the new candidate and retain prior results as historical evidence.\n5. **Stable promotion and publication.** After exact-RC acceptance and G6 pass, create a stable-version commit directly on the accepted RC commit, changing only the synchronized version metadata required for v1.0.0. Record both commits and the version-only diff. Run required source CI and all applicable source G0–G4 checks on this exact stable-version commit before tagging. If any other source changes, restart RC construction and qualification. Then create the `v1.0.0` tag on that checked commit and build a draft release. Keep its assets unpublished while verifying the stable artifact identity, provenance, package contents, installation, and publication metadata. Compare the stable package contents with the accepted RC; if packaged behavior or content differs beyond version-specific metadata, rerun affected installed-RC qualification gates and obtain independent acceptance for the stable artifact. Publish the draft only after these checks pass and the signed acceptance disposition is recorded. Record the published digests and provenance.\n\nThe final G6 decision in §10 applies to promoting the accepted RC toward a stable release. It requires the applicable gates to pass for the accepted RC and authorizes the version-only stable commit and its source checks, followed by the stable tag and draft build; it does not waive source CI, artifact identity, installed-RC qualification, or any threshold. Public publication is a separate post-build gate in stage 5 above. The release workflow must keep stable assets in draft state until stable artifact checks pass. Source verification at integration 6a16cb6 confirms the tag-triggered release workflow now supports published RC prereleases and draft stable releases. The earlier immediate-stable-publication source blocker is resolved; an actual stable draft build, artifact verification, signed acceptance, and deliberate publication are still required and remain unproven until their run evidence is recorded.\n\nExact-RC requirements records identify at least one implementer and an independent reviewer. For a blocking requirement recorded as `tested` instead of `product-qualified`, the cited decision must be explicitly allowlisted for that requirement in the approved qualification-exceptions table below, and its `DECISIONS.md` section must contain a matching requirement-specific line of the form `Qualification applicability: REQ-... = NOT REQUIRED ...`. A record-supplied or unrelated decision cannot authorize an exception. The record must include digest-bound evidence for every `ACC-*` criterion in that requirement's ledger Acceptance column.\n\nThe post-RC closure requirements listed below may remain `open`, `partial`, or `implemented` in the source ledger at the RC commit because their remaining acceptance is completed during G6 or installed-RC qualification. The bare `--enforce` invocation is the pre-RC source gate: it checks every other B/RR row against the source threshold and reports mapped rows as deferred, not accepted. At G6, the exact-RC invocation must provide a record meeting the normal B/RR status threshold and digest-bound evidence for every criterion in this table. The table also supplies the criterion mapping for rows whose ledger points to an ACCEPTANCE section instead of naming ACC IDs. The source ledger is never rewritten by the record. Any below-tested required row omitted from this table still fails.\n\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-ONLY:START -->\n### Qualification-only requirements for exact-RC enforcement\n\n| Requirement | Acceptance criteria required in the exact-RC record | Remaining stage |\n|---|---|---|\n| REQ-REL-003 | ACC-PLAT | Installed-RC platform and Node-version matrix |\n| REQ-AGT-002 | ACC-VER-03, ACC-VER-04, ACC-VER-09, ACC-QUAL-T2, ACC-QUAL-T3, ACC-QUAL-T4 | Installed-RC T2/T3/T4 outcome thresholds |\n| REQ-AGT-010 | ACC-TRUST-04 | Independent acceptance of the DEC-04 v1 subset |\n| REQ-PRV-003 | ACC-INF-10 | Installed-RC standalone and absent-gateway behavior |\n| REQ-REL-005 | ACC-SUP-04, ACC-SUP-05 | Exact-RC security and dependency artifact checks |\n| REQ-AGT-110 | ACC-CONT-10, ACC-GOV-06 | Installed-RC continuity and independent acceptance |\n| REQ-PRV-111 | ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC provider verification |\n| REQ-REL-114 | ACC-SUP-08 | Exact-RC cross-runner reproducibility |\n| REQ-QUAL-101 | ACC-QUAL-T2 | Installed-RC held-out T2 qualification |\n| REQ-QUAL-107 | ACC-PRV-STANDALONE, ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC standalone and provider matrix |\n| REQ-QUAL-108 | ACC-TUI-STEER, ACC-TUI-A11Y | Installed-RC automated and scripted PTY acceptance; the manual E3 matrix is waived by DEC-20 |\n| REQ-QUAL-109 | ACC-QUAL-T3 | Installed-RC T3 long-task qualification |\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-ONLY:END -->\n\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-EXCEPTIONS:START -->\n### Approved exact-RC qualification exceptions\n\n| Requirement | Approved decision | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-EXCEPTIONS:END -->\n\n## 1. Rules\n\n1. The RC is built and published by the release workflow from a clean tag. Its version is identical across all components (REQ-REL-103).\n2. All qualification runs use the **installed RC package**, installed with the documented one-line command. They never use a checkout build. The only exception is CI unit and PTY suites, which run from source at the same commit.\n3. Thresholds (DEC-05) are frozen before the first M5 run. A failing gate is fixed in code and the run is repeated as `rc.N+1`. A threshold is never relaxed after results are known.\n4. Evidence goes to `docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/<rc>/`. The directory contains:\n   - `manifest.json`: commit, package sha256, environment, model fingerprint, dates;\n   - raw outputs;\n   - a `SHA256SUMS` file.\n\n   Secrets are redacted before commit.\n5. Any step that cannot run in the available environment is recorded as **NOT RUN** with its reason. A NOT RUN on a blocker gate fails the gate.\n\n## 2. Environments\n\n| ID | Environment | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | GitHub Actions: ubuntu-22.04 and ubuntu-24.04; Node 22 and 24; pinned Rust | Unit, integration and PTY suites; packaging; reproducibility |\n| E2 | Docker clean images: ubuntu:22.04, ubuntu:24.04, debian:12, fedora:40. Each with no Node, then with Node 20, Node 22 and Node 24. Non-root user. | Installer, upgrade, rollback, uninstall, platform failure messages |\n| E3 | Operator dev box: Linux x86-64, real terminals (xterm, kitty, tmux, VS Code terminal, GNOME Terminal) | Terminal matrix, live sessions |\n| E4 | Homelab-ai gateway, the **only** inference path (DEC-16): direct, and through `eval/standalone/plain_proxy.mjs` | Task-quality runs, standalone qualification, T3/T4 |\n| E5 | Cloud, small spend: ChatGPT sign-in (experimental tier) and OpenRouter with a pinned model; OpenAI and Anthropic keys if added | Provider verify; one live task per supported cloud provider |\n\n## 3. Gate checklist (G0–G4: automated acceptance IDs)\n\nEvery ACC ID below is defined in the linked spec. The plan for each spec names the test that implements it. Run each check at the lifecycle stage dictated by its acceptance method in §1.1: source-applicable checks before the RC tag; checks requiring the RC archive, attestation, release, or installed package after RC construction; environment-specific checks in their declared environment. Bind every result to the exact source commit and, when artifact-dependent, the RC package digest. A source test or local build does not replace an artifact-bound check, and no G0–G4 ID is complete until all of its applicable checks pass.\n\n| Gate | Acceptance IDs | Spec |\n|---|---|---|\n| Safety | ACC-SAFE-01 to ACC-SAFE-10 | SPEC-01 |\n| Process ownership | ACC-PROC-01 to ACC-PROC-08 (ACC-PROC-07 is the load test, run in E3) | SPEC-02 |\n| Untrusted inputs | ACC-TRUST-01 to ACC-TRUST-09 | SPEC-03 |\n| Continuity | ACC-CONT-01 to ACC-CONT-09 (ACC-CONT-10 is in §8) | SPEC-04 |\n| Inference | ACC-INF-01 to ACC-INF-09 (ACC-INF-10 is in §4) | SPEC-05 |\n| Packaging | ACC-PKG-01 to ACC-PKG-09 | SPEC-06 |\n| Supply chain | ACC-SUP-01 to ACC-SUP-08 (artifact-bound ACC-SUP-01/02/03/06/08 after RC construction) | SPEC-07 |\n| Persistence | ACC-PER-01 to ACC-PER-07 | SPEC-08 |\n| Governance | ACC-GOV-01 to ACC-GOV-06 | SPEC-09 |\n| Verification | ACC-VER-01 to ACC-VER-09 | SPEC-11 |\n| Upgrade | ACC-UPG-01 to ACC-UPG-14 | SPEC-12 |\n\n\n### 3.1 Acceptance IDs defined in this file\n\nThese are retained commitments from REQUIREMENTS §2 and cross-cutting checks.\n\n| ID | Check | Pass condition |\n|---|---|---|\n| ACC-TUI-STEER | PTY: submit a task; while a tool runs, type steering text and press Enter; also queue a steer before a cancel | Steering is applied at the next step boundary. Unapplied steering is returned to the composer. Nothing is dropped silently (existing `sidecar_steering_diff` plus PTY s6). |\n| ACC-TUI-A11Y | PTY with `NO_COLOR=1` and with `TERM=dumb` at 40×14 and 100×30 | Zero colour SGR codes. Every state is distinguishable by glyph and word. Hints are complete at 40 columns (TUI-10). |\n| ACC-SEC-JAIL | `core/tests` plus harness-core calls with `..`, absolute, symlinked-file, symlinked-dir and symlinked-parent payloads | Every one is refused (C §4 R1 set). |\n| ACC-SEC-B6 | Re-run `docs/reviews/evidence/qualification_2026-10-12/repro/zz_trust_repro.test.ts`. Seeded-secret scan of `~/.nexus` and `.nexus` after a scripted turn. | Zero plaintext seeded secrets in durable stores or outbound bodies. |\n| ACC-PRV-LIVE | `nexus providers verify --provider <p> -m <pinned model> -j` for each provider listed as supported (DEC-06) | `passed: true`. Receipts archived. |\n| ACC-PRV-STANDALONE | §4.2 on the installed RC through the approved stripped proxy, with gateway credentials absent from the child environment | Every required profile completes through the proxy and the absent-gateway boundary holds; receipts archived. |\n| ACC-PLAT | E2 matrix, §7 | All rows pass. |\n| ACC-QUAL-T2 | §6.1 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n| ACC-QUAL-T3 | §6.3 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n| ACC-QUAL-T4 | §6.2 + §6.4 | Thresholds met. |\n| ACC-QUAL-SMOKE | `make test` includes the T1 self-test and the T4 smoke | Present and passing. |\n| ACC-TUI-MANUAL | §5.3 on the installed RC across five terminal environments and three sizes | Waived for v1.0.0 by DEC-20. No manual-cell execution or evidence is required, and no result is inferred. Automated §5.1 and scripted PTY §5.2 remain mandatory. |\n\n## 4. Standalone and provider qualification (REQ-PRV-106, REQ-PRV-111, REQ-QUAL-107)\n\n### 4.1 Clean-HOME procedure\n\n1. `export HOME=$(mktemp -d)`.\n2. Install the RC with the documented command.\n3. Create `~/.config/nexus/config.env` containing only:\n   - `LOCAL_LLM_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:<proxy>/v1`\n   - `LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=<id as listed by the proxy>`\n   - `LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY=REDACTED\n\n   No gateway token file, no homelab paths, no `NODE_*` overrides.\n4. Start `node eval/standalone/plain_proxy.mjs --upstream <gateway> --token-file <operator token> [--emulate <kind>]`. The gateway token stays with the proxy and never reaches the Nexus HOME.\n5. Run `nexus doctor --json` and archive it. It must report the server kind and context source with no `gateway` capability.\n\n### 4.2 Standalone matrix\n\n| Profile | What Nexus sees | Runs | Pass condition |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P-plain | Bare OpenAI-compatible `/models` and chat. No tokenize, no capacity headers. | Standalone T2 subset (≥ 10 held-out tasks) × 3 runs; T4 classes 1, 3, 4, 7 × 3 | T2 correctness ≥ (gateway-direct T2 correctness on the same tasks − 10 pts). False VERIFIED = 0. T4 truthful = 100%. Context source reported as `client_default` or an operator override, with no error. |\n| P-llamacpp | Emulated llama.cpp `/props` and overflow body | Same tasks × 1 run; plus the forced-overflow case (context limit set to 8k) | Context discovered from `/props`. Overflow gives one compact-and-retry, then success or an actionable error. |\n| P-vllm | Emulated `max_model_len` and vLLM overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `max_model_len` |\n| P-ollama | Emulated `/api/show` and Ollama overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `ollama_show` |\n| Conformance | CI fixtures for llama.cpp, vLLM, LM Studio and Ollama shapes | CI | All pass (ACC-INF-01, -02, -05) |\n\nACC-INF-10 passes only when every row passes. The release notes state the scope in the form required by DEC-16.\n\n### 4.3 Provider matrix (E5)\n\n| Provider | Tier (DEC-06) | Required for v1.0.0 |\n|---|---|---|\n| OpenRouter, pinned model | Supported | `providers verify` PASS, plus one live small task (a §6-style bug fix) reaching a correct outcome |\n| ChatGPT sign-in | Experimental | Verify PASS recorded (already PASS at db1ca55; repeat on the RC) |\n| OpenAI API key, Anthropic | Experimental unless keys are added | If promoted: verify PASS plus one live task. Otherwise NOT RUN, and they are labelled experimental. |\n| Copilot | Experimental | Not required |\n\n## 5. Terminal acceptance (REQ-QUAL-108)\n\n### 5.1 CI PTY suites (E1)\n\nThese must pass:\n\n- `tui_approval_input_e2e`\n- `tui_diff_e2e`\n- `tui_maturity_e2e`\n- `tui_model_picker_e2e`\n- `tui_multiline_e2e`\n- `tui_surface_e2e`\n- new: `repl_approval_e2e`\n- new: `process_lifecycle_e2e`\n- new: `startup_unreachable_e2e`\n- new: the package PTY smoke\n\n### 5.2 Scripted PTY scenarios against the installed RC (E3, mock gateway)\n\nThese are ports of `evidence/D_pty/`.\n\n| Scenario | Pass condition |\n|---|---|\n| s1 startup, unreachable endpoint | The endpoint is named, with a fix hint. The turn ends with guidance in ≤ 2.5 min. |\n| s2 approval under continuous typing, paste and burst, at 40×14 | No decision is made by stray input. A deliberate key decides. |\n| s3 completion | The outcome is shown in words: VERIFIED, REGRESSION_FREE or UNVERIFIED. |\n| s4–s6 cancel and steer | Cancel is acknowledged within 1 frame and idle within 5 s. Steering is applied. |\n| s7 resume | The prior conversation is shown. |\n| s8–s9 cancel or quit during a tool | Zero surviving processes. |\n| r1a / r1d REPL approvals | DENIED for a lone Enter and for a typed-ahead `y`. |\n\n### 5.3 Manual real-terminal matrix (E3)\n\n**Waived for v1.0.0 by DEC-20.** The five-terminal by three-size manual matrix below is not a release gate; no operator runs, cell verdicts, or per-cell evidence are required, and none are claimed. Automated CI checks in §5.1 and scripted installed-RC PTY scenarios in §5.2 remain mandatory and must pass.\n\nTerminals: xterm, kitty (kitty keyboard protocol on), tmux 3.x inside GNOME Terminal, VS Code integrated terminal, GNOME Terminal. Sizes: 80×24, 40×14, 200×60.\n\nFor each terminal × size, check:\n\n1. Launch `nexus` (the TUI appears).\n2. Approval panel. Type continuously across it. Paste 3 lines. Approve with a deliberate key.\n3. Shift+Enter or Alt+Enter multiline.\n4. Resize during streaming.\n5. Esc during `sleep 30` (no survivors).\n6. Ctrl-C twice (clean terminal restore: echo on, no stray escape codes).\n7. NO_COLOR.\n\nRecord PASS/FAIL per cell and the observed outcomes for each check, bound to the exact installed RC artifact, terminal environment, and size. Screenshot artifacts are waived for v1.0.0 per [DEC-18](DECISIONS.md#dec-18-v100-terminal-screenshot-artifact-waiver); this does not waive any interactive check, safety gate, per-cell verdict, or evidence record.\n\n**Gate:** zero FAIL on checks 2, 5 and 6, which are safety checks. Other FAILs need a written disposition (fix, or a documented known limitation).\n\n## 6. Task-quality qualification (REQ-QUAL-101 to REQ-QUAL-106, REQ-QUAL-109)\n# Nexus Code v1.0.0-rc.14 release candidate\n\n**Maturity:** release candidate; prerelease, not stable.\n\n**Status:** this candidate is for exact-artifact qualification. It does not establish independent acceptance or authorize stable v1.0.0 promotion. Qualification results must identify the workflow-produced package and its digest.\n\nThis release packages the Nexus Code Linux x86-64 client: the TypeScript CLI and sidecar, production Node dependencies, Rust `harness-core`, and Ratatui `nexus-tui`. It replaces RC12 and RC13, which were tagged but never published: both release workflows failed in the test step, so neither package exists. RC14 ships the same product changes as RC12; everything in the [RC13](v1.0.0-rc.13.md), [RC12](v1.0.0-rc.12.md) and [RC11](v1.0.0-rc.11.md) notes is included.\n\n## Changes since RC13\n\n### Qualification tooling and release workflow (not in the package)\n\n- The evaluator's compiler-exposure check no longer depends on deleting the hosted runner's preinstalled SDK trees. It masks host-only SDK directories inside the candidate sandbox under every mounted spelling, ignores an inventoried path only when it has been removed before inspection, and names the cause when its inventory helper fails (timeout, kill, nonzero exit or unexpected output). Every other inspection error still refuses qualification. RC13's release run failed when this check refused in two evaluator tests.\n- The CLI test step in the release and CI workflows now has a 45-minute limit. RC13's release run and a main CI run stalled after the evaluator test file finished, because its test process did not exit; a hung step now fails the job instead of running for six hours. The limit bounds the damage; the cause of the non-exiting test process has not been identified.\n\nNo product code changed between RC12's tag and RC14.\n\n## Runtime requirements\n\n- Linux x86-64 with glibc 2.35 or newer.\n- Node.js 22 or newer.\n- An OpenAI-compatible inference endpoint, local or explicitly selected provider.\n\nThe archive does not include Node.js. Rust and Cargo are not required at runtime. No other OS or architecture is claimed.\n\n## Candidate assets\n\nThe release workflow for `v1.0.0-rc.14` is expected to produce the version-matched Linux archive, `SHA256SUMS`, SBOM, notices, installer, and provenance. The workflow run, tag commit, artifact digests, and qualification evidence must be recorded before this candidate can be accepted. Local builds are not interchangeable with the workflow-produced artifacts.\n\n## Qualification status\n\nThis is a candidate, not an accepted release. The installed-RC gates — platform and lifecycle evidence including the power-loss cell, mandatory automated CI PTY (§5.1) and scripted installed-RC PTY (§5.2), provider and standalone qualification, held-out task quality, cross-runner reproducibility, independent exact-RC acceptance and the requirements ledger — remain outstanding for this artifact. RC11 evidence does not carry over to RC14 without an explicit, reviewed carry-forward. The manual matrix (§5.3) is waived for v1.0.0 by DEC-20; this does not waive steering, accessibility, or any other release gate. Passing source CI does not satisfy installed-RC qualification.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":50043}},"started_at_ms":1793353510988,"completed_at_ms":1793353510988}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T09:45:11.783Z","ordinal":526922,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18c20-aec5-7552-93bd-755d714c245e","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-d56756f2-39f9-4313-ab4d-479834c273e2","process_id":"36116","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","gh pr view 334 --json number,title,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus,statusCheckRollup,url,body | jq '{number,title,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus,statusCheckRollup:[.statusCheckRollup[]|{name,conclusion,status}],url,body:(.body|.[0:1800])}'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"gh pr view 334 --json number,title,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus,statusCheckRollup,url,body | jq '{number,title,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefOid,reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus,statusCheckRollup:[.statusCheckRollup[]|{name,conclusion,status}],url,body:(.body|.[0:1800])}'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"number\": 334,\n  \"title\": \"evidence(e2): record the power-loss durability cell (fails on RC10)\",\n  \"state\": \"OPEN\",\n  \"headRefName\": \"v1/e2-powerloss-evidence-20261030\",\n  \"headRefOid\": \"f28263e0fa143f4b7ca3d7563ad38773076b8dd2\",\n  \"baseRefOid\": \"1acff70018c00c2459c388f34dfb2997b8b4d7c6\",\n  \"reviewDecision\": \"\",\n  \"mergeStateStatus\": \"UNKNOWN\",\n  \"statusCheckRollup\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"test-matrix (22)\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"SUCCESS\",\n      \"status\": \"COMPLETED\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"javascript-typescript\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"SUCCESS\",\n      \"status\": \"COMPLETED\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"test-matrix (24)\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"SUCCESS\",\n      \"status\": \"COMPLETED\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"linux-x86_64\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"FAILURE\",\n      \"status\": \"COMPLETED\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Entire Gates\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"FAILURE\",\n      \"status\": \"COMPLETED\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"CodeQL\",\n      \"conclusion\": \"SUCCESS\",\n      \"status\": \"COMPLETED\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": null,\n      \"conclusion\": null,\n      \"status\": null\n    }\n  ],\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/334\",\n  \"body\": \"Records the last open E2 lifecycle cell — and it **fails on RC10**.\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\n`lifecycle/powerloss/` builds a 2 GiB ext4 image with published v1.0.0-rc.9 installed, boots a minimal QEMU/KVM guest, starts `nexus upgrade --version v1.0.0-rc.10`, and `SIGKILL`s the VM when the durable journal reaches `switched` (package in place, launcher switched, runtime verification not yet run). Nothing is synced first; the image is mounted `cache=none`, so dirty page cache is lost exactly as in a power cut.\\n\\nThe second boot dumps the journal, upgrade log and package directories, prints each package's `bin/nexus` size, parses every JSON state file, runs `nexus upgrade --recover`, and asserts rollback, staging removal, stale-lock reclamation and idempotence, then retries the upgrade to completion.\\n\\n## Result on the published RC10 artifact: FAIL\\n\\n- `payload v1.0.0-rc.10: 0 bytes .../bin/nexus` (rc.9 payload: 580 bytes)\\n- `nexus --version` prints nothing\\n- `nexus upgrade --recover` **exits 0 without doing anything**\\n\\nA 0-byte `#!/bin/sh` launcher is an empty shell script, so recovery silently succeeds as a no-op; an automation check on the exit status would read the failed recovery as success. The upgrade is performed by the installed rc.9 CLI, and the missing fsync is in that running CLI.\\n\\n## Fix demonstration (not qualification)\\n\\n`NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` runs the same published artifacts through a CLI containing the fix; the guest records the SHA-256 of that dist's sorted file manifest (covering `durable_tree.js`, where the fix lives). That cut leaves a working launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, staging is removed, the stale lock is reclaimed, a second recovery is a no-op, and the retry completes to rc.10.\\n\\nThis is explicitly **not** a pass for RC10. The cell can o\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":764720714}},"started_at_ms":1793353511001,"completed_at_ms":1793353511772}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T09:45:12.208Z","ordinal":526923,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a18c32-f510-7e02-a2c1-5d03492c4d09","call_id":"call_df38d73d74f8422f9b0b754630f82b34","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.6 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"1bbc0f\",\"wall_time_seconds\":1.001225491,\"session_id\":48814,\"original_token_count\":0,\"output\":\"\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"0941d8\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000050043,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":5458,\"output\":\"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 5458)\\nTotal output lines: 242\\n\\n# Nexus Code v1.0.0: release qualification procedure\\n\\nThis is the procedure that decides whether a release candidate (RC) may become v1.0.0. It defines:\\n\\n- the environments;\\n- the workflow matrix;\\n- the evidence each step must produce;\\n- explicit pass/fail gates.\\n\\nThree separate questions are judged and recorded separately:\\n\\n| Question | Who answers it |\\n|---|---|\\n| **Implementation self-assessment** | Did the implementer's tests pass? |\\n| **Product qualification** | Did the RC meet these gates on real workflows? |\\n| **Independent acceptance** | Did a reviewer other than the implementer agree? |\\n\\nA gate passes only on archived evidence bound to the source commit or installed RC artifact that the gate evaluates. Source-level evidence names the exact source commit; installed-RC evidence names both that commit and the installed package digest. A green test suite alone never qualifies the product.\\n\\n## 1.1 Release-candidate lifecycle and evidence identity\\n\\nThe release sequence has five distinct stages. Passing an earlier stage is a prerequisite for the next stage, not a substitute for it. Every G0–G4 acceptance ID remains mandatory; the stage determines which exact input and evidence it evaluates, not whether the check applies.\\n\\n1. **Pre-RC source gates.** Before creating an RC tag, run required source CI and every G0–G4 check whose acceptance method evaluates source, a source-built test binary, or an environment without requiring a published RC. Record the full commit ID and check results. Source checks do not establish behavior of the installed package.\\n2. **RC artifact construction.** After the required owner authorization, create the version-matched RC tag on that exact source commit and use the repository release workflow to build and publish the RC artifacts. Record the workflow run, tag commit, artifact digests, and provenance. The workflow-produced artifacts are the candidates for qualification; a local build or a different commit is not interchangeable. RC artifact publication is not product qualification or final release acceptance.\\n3. **RC artifact gates.** Before installed-RC product qualification, run every G0–G4 check whose acceptance method requires the RC archive, RC attestation/release, or workflow-produced artifact. This includes ACC-SUP-01/02/03/06/08. ACC-PKG checks that explicitly require an installed package run against this RC as specified by their test method. Record the RC tag commit, workflow run, artifact digests, and each check result. A source-level analogue does not replace an artifact-bound check.\\n4. **Installed-RC qualification.** Install the workflow-produced RC package using the documented procedure and run the applicable product, provider, task-quality, terminal, installation, and independent-acceptance gates in §§4–8. Bind each result to both the RC source commit and installed package digest. If a source change or rebuilt artifact changes either identity, rerun every affected gate against the new candidate and retain prior results as historical evidence.\\n5. **Stable promotion and publication.** After exact-RC acceptance and G6 pass, create a stable-version commit directly on the accepted RC commit, changing only the synchronized version metadata required for v1.0.0. Record both commits and the version-only diff. Run required source CI and all applicable source G0–G4 checks on this exact stable-version commit before tagging. If any other source changes, restart RC construction and qualification. Then create the `v1.0.0` tag on that checked commit and build a draft release. Keep its assets unpublished while verifying the stable artifact identity, provenance, package contents, installation, and publication metadata. Compare the stable package contents with the accepted RC; if packaged behavior or content differs beyond version-specific metadata, rerun affected installed-RC qualification gates and obtain independent acceptance for the stable artifact. Publish the draft only after these checks pass and the signed acceptance disposition is recorded. Record the published digests and provenance.\\n\\nThe final G6 decision in §10 applies to promoting the accepted RC toward a stable release. It requires the applicable gates to pass for the accepted RC and authorizes the version-only stable commit and its source checks, followed by the stable tag and draft build; it does not waive source CI, artifact identity, installed-RC qualification, or any threshold. Public publication is a separate post-build gate in stage 5 above. The release workflow must keep stable assets in draft state until stable artifact checks pass. Source verification at integration 6a16cb6 confirms the tag-triggered release workflow now supports published RC prereleases and draft stable releases. The earlier immediate-stable-publication source blocker is resolved; an actual stable draft build, artifact verification, signed acceptance, and deliberate publication are still required and remain unproven until their run evidence is recorded.\\n\\nExact-RC requirements records identify at least one implementer and an independent reviewer. For a blocking requirement recorded as `tested` instead of `product-qualified`, the cited decision must be explicitly allowlisted for that requirement in the approved qualification-exceptions table below, and its `DECISIONS.md` section must contain a matching requirement-specific line of the form `Qualification applicability: REQ-... = NOT REQUIRED ...`. A record-supplied or unrelated decision cannot authorize an exception. The record must include digest-bound evidence for every `ACC-*` criterion in that requirement's ledger Acceptance column.\\n\\nThe post-RC closure requirements listed below may remain `open`, `partial`, or `implemented` in the source ledger at the RC commit because their remaining acceptance is completed during G6 or installed-RC qualification. The bare `--enforce` invocation is the pre-RC source gate: it checks every other B/RR row against the source threshold and reports mapped rows as deferred, not accepted. At G6, the exact-RC invocation must provide a record meeting the normal B/RR status threshold and digest-bound evidence for every criterion in this table. The table also supplies the criterion mapping for rows whose ledger points to an ACCEPTANCE section instead of naming ACC IDs. The source ledger is never rewritten by the record. Any below-tested required row omitted from this table still fails.\\n\\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-ONLY:START -->\\n### Qualification-only requirements for exact-RC enforcement\\n\\n| Requirement | Acceptance criteria required in the exact-RC record | Remaining stage |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| REQ-REL-003 | ACC-PLAT | Installed-RC platform and Node-version matrix |\\n| REQ-AGT-002 | ACC-VER-03, ACC-VER-04, ACC-VER-09, ACC-QUAL-T2, ACC-QUAL-T3, ACC-QUAL-T4 | Installed-RC T2/T3/T4 outcome thresholds |\\n| REQ-AGT-010 | ACC-TRUST-04 | Independent acceptance of the DEC-04 v1 subset |\\n| REQ-PRV-003 | ACC-INF-10 | Installed-RC standalone and absent-gateway behavior |\\n| REQ-REL-005 | ACC-SUP-04, ACC-SUP-05 | Exact-RC security and dependency artifact checks |\\n| REQ-AGT-110 | ACC-CONT-10, ACC-GOV-06 | Installed-RC continuity and independent acceptance |\\n| REQ-PRV-111 | ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC provider verification |\\n| REQ-REL-114 | ACC-SUP-08 | Exact-RC cross-runner reproducibility |\\n| REQ-QUAL-101 | ACC-QUAL-T2 | Installed-RC held-out T2 qualification |\\n| REQ-QUAL-107 | ACC-PRV-STANDALONE, ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC standalone and provider matrix |\\n| REQ-QUAL-108 | ACC-TUI-STEER, ACC-TUI-A11Y | Installed-RC automated and scripted PTY acceptance; the manual E3 matrix is waived by DEC-20 |\\n| REQ-QUAL-109 | ACC-QUAL-T3 | Installed-RC T3 long-task qualification |\\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-ONLY:END -->\\n\\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-EXCEPTIONS:START -->\\n### Approved exact-RC qualification exceptions\\n\\n| Requirement | Approved decision | Scope |\\n|---|---|---|\\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-EXCEPTIONS:END -->\\n\\n## 1. Rules\\n\\n1. The RC is built and published by the release workflow from a clean tag. Its version is identical across all components (REQ-REL-103).\\n2. All qualification runs use the **installed RC package**, installed with the documented one-line command. They never use a checkout build. The only exception is CI unit and PTY suites, which run from source at the same commit.\\n3. Thresholds (DEC-05) are frozen before the fir…1258 tokens truncated…main mandatory. |\\n\\n## 4. Standalone and provider qualification (REQ-PRV-106, REQ-PRV-111, REQ-QUAL-107)\\n\\n### 4.1 Clean-HOME procedure\\n\\n1. `export HOME=$(mktemp -d)`.\\n2. Install the RC with the documented command.\\n3. Create `~/.config/nexus/config.env` containing only:\\n   - `LOCAL_LLM_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:<proxy>/v1`\\n   - `LOCAL_LLM_MODEL=<id as listed by the proxy>`\\n   - `LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY=REDACTED   No gateway token file, no homelab paths, no `NODE_*` overrides.\\n4. Start `node eval/standalone/plain_proxy.mjs --upstream <gateway> --token-file <operator token> [--emulate <kind>]`. The gateway token stays with the proxy and never reaches the Nexus HOME.\\n5. Run `nexus doctor --json` and archive it. It must report the server kind and context source with no `gateway` capability.\\n\\n### 4.2 Standalone matrix\\n\\n| Profile | What Nexus sees | Runs | Pass condition |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| P-plain | Bare OpenAI-compatible `/models` and chat. No tokenize, no capacity headers. | Standalone T2 subset (≥ 10 held-out tasks) × 3 runs; T4 classes 1, 3, 4, 7 × 3 | T2 correctness ≥ (gateway-direct T2 correctness on the same tasks − 10 pts). False VERIFIED = 0. T4 truthful = 100%. Context source reported as `client_default` or an operator override, with no error. |\\n| P-llamacpp | Emulated llama.cpp `/props` and overflow body | Same tasks × 1 run; plus the forced-overflow case (context limit set to 8k) | Context discovered from `/props`. Overflow gives one compact-and-retry, then success or an actionable error. |\\n| P-vllm | Emulated `max_model_len` and vLLM overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `max_model_len` |\\n| P-ollama | Emulated `/api/show` and Ollama overflow body | Same as P-llamacpp | Same, with the context source `ollama_show` |\\n| Conformance | CI fixtures for llama.cpp, vLLM, LM Studio and Ollama shapes | CI | All pass (ACC-INF-01, -02, -05) |\\n\\nACC-INF-10 passes only when every row passes. The release notes state the scope in the form required by DEC-16.\\n\\n### 4.3 Provider matrix (E5)\\n\\n| Provider | Tier (DEC-06) | Required for v1.0.0 |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| OpenRouter, pinned model | Supported | `providers verify` PASS, plus one live small task (a §6-style bug fix) reaching a correct outcome |\\n| ChatGPT sign-in | Experimental | Verify PASS recorded (already PASS at db1ca55; repeat on the RC) |\\n| OpenAI API key, Anthropic | Experimental unless keys are added | If promoted: verify PASS plus one live task. Otherwise NOT RUN, and they are labelled experimental. |\\n| Copilot | Experimental | Not required |\\n\\n## 5. Terminal acceptance (REQ-QUAL-108)\\n\\n### 5.1 CI PTY suites (E1)\\n\\nThese must pass:\\n\\n- `tui_approval_input_e2e`\\n- `tui_diff_e2e`\\n- `tui_maturity_e2e`\\n- `tui_model_picker_e2e`\\n- `tui_multiline_e2e`\\n- `tui_surface_e2e`\\n- new: `repl_approval_e2e`\\n- new: `process_lifecycle_e2e`\\n- new: `startup_unreachable_e2e`\\n- new: the package PTY smoke\\n\\n### 5.2 Scripted PTY scenarios against the installed RC (E3, mock gateway)\\n\\nThese are ports of `evidence/D_pty/`.\\n\\n| Scenario | Pass condition |\\n|---|---|\\n| s1 startup, unreachable endpoint | The endpoint is named, with a fix hint. The turn ends with guidance in ≤ 2.5 min. |\\n| s2 approval under continuous typing, paste and burst, at 40×14 | No decision is made by stray input. A deliberate key decides. |\\n| s3 completion | The outcome is shown in words: VERIFIED, REGRESSION_FREE or UNVERIFIED. |\\n| s4–s6 cancel and steer | Cancel is acknowledged within 1 frame and idle within 5 s. Steering is applied. |\\n| s7 resume | The prior conversation is shown. |\\n| s8–s9 cancel or quit during a tool | Zero surviving processes. |\\n| r1a / r1d REPL approvals | DENIED for a lone Enter and for a typed-ahead `y`. |\\n\\n### 5.3 Manual real-terminal matrix (E3)\\n\\n**Waived for v1.0.0 by DEC-20.** The five-terminal by three-size manual matrix below is not a release gate; no operator runs, cell verdicts, or per-cell evidence are required, and none are claimed. Automated CI checks in §5.1 and scripted installed-RC PTY scenarios in §5.2 remain mandatory and must pass.\\n\\nTerminals: xterm, kitty (kitty keyboard protocol on), tmux 3.x inside GNOME Terminal, VS Code integrated terminal, GNOME Terminal. Sizes: 80×24, 40×14, 200×60.\\n\\nFor each terminal × size, check:\\n\\n1. Launch `nexus` (the TUI appears).\\n2. Approval panel. Type continuously across it. Paste 3 lines. Approve with a deliberate key.\\n3. Shift+Enter or Alt+Enter multiline.\\n4. Resize during streaming.\\n5. Esc during `sleep 30` (no survivors).\\n6. Ctrl-C twice (clean terminal restore: echo on, no stray escape codes).\\n7. NO_COLOR.\\n\\nRecord PASS/FAIL per cell and the observed outcomes for each check, bound to the exact installed RC artifact, terminal environment, and size. Screenshot artifacts are waived for v1.0.0 per [DEC-18](DECISIONS.md#dec-18-v100-terminal-screenshot-artifact-waiver); this does not waive any interactive check, safety gate, per-cell verdict, or evidence record.\\n\\n**Gate:** zero FAIL on checks 2, 5 and 6, which are safety checks. Other FAILs need a written disposition (fix, or a documented known limitation).\\n\\n## 6. Task-quality qualification (REQ-QUAL-101 to REQ-QUAL-106, REQ-QUAL-109)\\n# Nexus Code v1.0.0-rc.14 release candidate\\n\\n**Maturity:** release candidate; prerelease, not stable.\\n\\n**Status:** this candidate is for exact-artifact qualification. It does not establish independent acceptance or authorize stable v1.0.0 promotion. Qualification results must identify the workflow-produced package and its digest.\\n\\nThis release packages the Nexus Code Linux x86-64 client: the TypeScript CLI and sidecar, production Node dependencies, Rust `harness-core`, and Ratatui `nexus-tui`. It replaces RC12 and RC13, which were tagged but never published: both release workflows failed in the test step, so neither package exists. RC14 ships the same product changes as RC12; everything in the [RC13](v1.0.0-rc.13.md), [RC12](v1.0.0-rc.12.md) and [RC11](v1.0.0-rc.11.md) notes is included.\\n\\n## Changes since RC13\\n\\n### Qualification tooling and release workflow (not in the package)\\n\\n- The evaluator's compiler-exposure check no longer depends on deleting the hosted runner's preinstalled SDK trees. It masks host-only SDK directories inside the candidate sandbox under every mounted spelling, ignores an inventoried path only when it has been removed before inspection, and names the cause when its inventory helper fails (timeout, kill, nonzero exit or unexpected output). Every other inspection error still refuses qualification. RC13's release run failed when this check refused in two evaluator tests.\\n- The CLI test step in the release and CI workflows now has a 45-minute limit. RC13's release run and a main CI run stalled after the evaluator test file finished, because its test process did not exit; a hung step now fails the job instead of running for six hours. The limit bounds the damage; the cause of the non-exiting test process has not been identified.\\n\\nNo product code changed between RC12's tag and RC14.\\n\\n## Runtime requirements\\n\\n- Linux x86-64 with glibc 2.35 or newer.\\n- Node.js 22 or newer.\\n- An OpenAI-compatible inference endpoint, local or explicitly selected provider.\\n\\nThe archive does not include Node.js. Rust and Cargo are not required at runtime. No other OS or architecture is claimed.\\n\\n## Candidate assets\\n\\nThe release workflow for `v1.0.0-rc.14` is expected to produce the version-matched Linux archive, `SHA256SUMS`, SBOM, notices, installer, and provenance. The workflow run, tag commit, artifact digests, and qualification evidence must be recorded before this candidate can be accepted. Local builds are not interchangeable with the workflow-produced artifacts.\\n\\n## Qualification status\\n\\nThis is a candidate, not an accepted release. The installed-RC gates — platform and lifecycle evidence including the power-loss cell, mandatory automated CI PTY (§5.1) and scripted installed-RC PTY (§5.2), provider and standalone qualification, held-out task quality, cross-runner reproducibility, independent exact-RC acceptance and the requirements ledger — remain outstanding for this artifact. RC11 evidence does not carry over to RC14 without an explicit, reviewed carry-forward. The manual matrix (§5.3) is waived for v1.0.0 by DEC-20; this does not waive steering, accessibility, or any other release gate. Passing source CI does not satisfy installed-RC qualification.\\n\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"f8f8be\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.764087541,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":747,\"output\":\"{\\n  \\\"number\\\": 334,\\n  \\\"title\\\": \\\"evidence(e2): record the power-loss durability cell (fails on RC10)\\\",\\n  \\\"state\\\": \\\"OPEN\\\",\\n  \\\"headRefName\\\": \\\"v1/e2-powerloss-evidence-20261030\\\",\\n  \\\"headRefOid\\\": \\\"f28263e0fa143f4b7ca3d7563ad38773076b8dd2\\\",\\n  \\\"baseRefOid\\\": \\\"1acff70018c00c2459c388f34dfb2997b8b4d7c6\\\",\\n  \\\"reviewDecision\\\": \\\"\\\",\\n  \\\"mergeStateStatus\\\": \\\"UNKNOWN\\\",\\n  \\\"statusCheckRollup\\\": [\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"test-matrix (22)\\\",\\n      \\\"conclusion\\\": \\\"SUCCESS\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"COMPLETED\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"javascript-typescript\\\",\\n      \\\"conclusion\\\": \\\"SUCCESS\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"COMPLETED\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"test-matrix (24)\\\",\\n      \\\"conclusion\\\": \\\"SUCCESS\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"COMPLETED\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"linux-x86_64\\\",\\n      \\\"conclusion\\\": \\\"FAILURE\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"COMPLETED\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"Entire Gates\\\",\\n      \\\"conclusion\\\": \\\"FAILURE\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"COMPLETED\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": \\\"CodeQL\\\",\\n      \\\"conclusion\\\": \\\"SUCCESS\\\",\\n      \\\"status\\\": \\\"COMPLETED\\\"\\n    },\\n    {\\n      \\\"name\\\": null,\\n      \\\"conclusion\\\": null,\\n      \\\"status\\\": null\\n    }\\n  ],\\n  \\\"url\\\": \\\"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/334\\\",\\n  \\\"body\\\": \\\"Records the last open E2 lifecycle cell — and it **fails on RC10**.\\\\n\\\\n## What was tested\\\\n\\\\n`lifecycle/powerloss/` builds a 2 GiB ext4 image with published v1.0.0-rc.9 installed, boots a minimal QEMU/KVM guest, starts `nexus upgrade --version v1.0.0-rc.10`, and `SIGKILL`s the VM when the durable journal reaches `switched` (package in place, launcher switched, runtime verification not yet run). Nothing is synced first; the image is mounted `cache=none`, so dirty page cache is lost exactly as in a power cut.\\\\n\\\\nThe second boot dumps the journal, upgrade log and package directories, prints each package's `bin/nexus` size, parses every JSON state file, runs `nexus upgrade --recover`, and asserts rollback, staging removal, stale-lock reclamation and idempotence, then retries the upgrade to completion.\\\\n\\\\n## Result on the published RC10 artifact: FAIL\\\\n\\\\n- `payload v1.0.0-rc.10: 0 bytes .../bin/nexus` (rc.9 payload: 580 bytes)\\\\n- `nexus --version` prints nothing\\\\n- `nexus upgrade --recover` **exits 0 without doing anything**\\\\n\\\\nA 0-byte `#!/bin/sh` launcher is an empty shell script, so recovery silently succeeds as a no-op; an automation check on the exit status would read the failed recovery as success. The upgrade is performed by the installed rc.9 CLI, and the missing fsync is in that running CLI.\\\\n\\\\n## Fix demonstration (not qualification)\\\\n\\\\n`NEXUS_POWERLOSS_BASELINE_DIST` runs the same published artifacts through a CLI containing the fix; the guest records the SHA-256 of that dist's sorted file manifest (covering `durable_tree.js`, where the fix lives). That cut leaves a working launcher, recovery rolls back to rc.9, staging is removed, the stale lock is reclaimed, a second recovery is a no-op, and the retry completes to rc.10.\\\\n\\\\nThis is explicitly **not** a pass for RC10. The cell can o\\\"\\n}\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18c20-aec5-7552-93bd-755d714c245e","create_time":1793353512.2082424}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
