{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T10:02:11.805Z","ordinal":527529,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_0db389702a7164cc016ae46b21b1c4819faee1cea7480e4646","status":"completed","call_id":"call_a0553a4e7c2948dbbf8475107de443b9","name":"exec","input":"const rs = await Promise.allSettled([\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"gh release view v1.0.0-rc.14 --json tagName,name,isDraft,isPrerelease,targetCommitish,url,assets | jq '{tagName,name,isDraft,isPrerelease,targetCommitish,url,assets:[.assets[]|{name,size,digest,state,url}]}'\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":2500}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md | rg -n -A8 -B5 'release|claim|owner.json|SUITE'; git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | sed -n '1,85p'\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":3500}),\n  tools.exec_command({cmd:\"gh run view 41035120551 --json status,conclusion,headSha,jobs,url | jq '{status,conclusion,headSha,url,jobs:[.jobs[]|{name,conclusion,steps:[.steps[]|select(.conclusion==\\\"failure\\\")|{name,conclusion}]}]}'\",\"workdir\":\"/home/mike/nexus-code\",\"yield_time_ms\":10000,\"max_output_tokens\":1000})\n]); for (const r of rs) text(r.status===\"fulfilled\"?r.value.output:String(r.reason));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a18c3d-7cdd-71d3-966d-f30f05df4fb0","create_time":1793354529.6987646}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T10:02:11.878Z","ordinal":527530,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18c3d-7cdd-71d3-966d-f30f05df4fb0","session_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","root_turn_id":"01a18c3d-7cdd-71d3-966d-f30f05df4fb0","response_id":"resp_0db389702a7164cc016ae46b1e2e9c819faf0fe46a31126a1d","usage":{"input_tokens":164274,"cached_input_tokens":163328,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":722,"reasoning_output_tokens":314,"total_tokens":164996},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":3826885,"cached_input_tokens":3813376,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":5876,"reasoning_output_tokens":2840,"total_tokens":3832761},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":9954354839,"cached_input_tokens":9849397376,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":28519238,"reasoning_output_tokens":16768002,"total_tokens":9982874077}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-30T10:02:12.200Z","ordinal":527531,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a181ab-bb8d-7471-bcfe-7451f754e731","turn_id":"01a18c3d-7cdd-71d3-966d-f30f05df4fb0","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-2d8acedd-a823-48fb-ba74-8c5fc20321c7","process_id":"13823","command":["/usr/bin/zsh","-lc","git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md | rg -n -A8 -B5 'release|claim|owner.json|SUITE'; git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | sed -n '1,85p'"],"cwd":"file:///home/mike/nexus-code","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/CONCURRENCY.md | rg -n -A8 -B5 'release|claim|owner.json|SUITE'; git show 16eeb6ee:docs/v1.0.0/ACCEPTANCE.md | sed -n '1,85p'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"7-- `pkill -f` killed the agent's own shell;\n8-- HEAD moved during a pause.\n9-\n10-They also follow from audit finding PER-03: the test suite writes into the real `~/.nexus`.\n11-\n12:## 1. Unit of work: one claimed PR slice per agent\n13-\n14-- The unit of concurrent work is **one PR slice** from a plan, for example `PLAN-01/PR-1a`. Every slice lists its tasks and its safe change boundary in its plan.\n15:- An agent works on exactly one claimed slice at a time.\n16-- An agent must not edit files outside its slice's boundary. The exceptions are the ledger rows and the shared files covered by §4. If the work needs a file outside the boundary, the agent stops and records a **boundary request** (§6). It does not edit the file.\n17-\n18-## 2. Claiming a slice (atomic and machine-local)\n19-\n20-Claims live in the repository's shared git directory. They are visible to every worktree, and they are never committed.\n21-\n22-```sh\n23:CLAIMS=\"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims\"\n24-mkdir -p \"$CLAIMS\"\n25-SLICE=\"PLAN-01_PR-1a\"\n26-if mkdir \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\" 2>/dev/null; then          # mkdir is atomic: exactly one agent wins\n27-  printf '{\"agent\":\"%s\",\"branch\":\"%s\",\"since\":\"%s\",\"pid\":%s}\\n' \\\n28:    \"$AGENT_NAME\" \"v1/plan-01-pr-1a\" \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ)\" \"$$\" > \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\"\n29-else\n30:  echo \"claimed by: $(cat \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE/owner.json\")\"; exit 1\n31-fi\n32-```\n33-\n34:- **Release** the claim by deleting its directory (`rm -r \"$CLAIMS/$SLICE\"`) after the slice merges or is abandoned. Write one line about the outcome to `$CLAIMS/../nexus-claims.log`.\n35:- **Hot-file claims** use the same mechanism with the name `FILE_<path with / replaced by _>`, for example `FILE_cli_src_agent.ts`. See §4.\n36:- **Stale claims.** A claim older than 24 h whose PID is gone may be broken, but only by the owner, or by an agent that first records the broken claim in the log.\n37:- If the MCP Agent Mail server is available, its file reservations (`file_reservation_paths`) may be used as well. The git-common-dir claim stays authoritative, because every agent can see it without extra tooling.\n38-\n39-## 3. Isolation: worktree, branch, HOME, ports, processes\n40-\n41-| Resource | Rule |\n42-|---|---|\n43:| Source | One `git worktree` per claimed slice: `git worktree add ../nexus-wt/<slice> -b v1/<plan>-<slice> origin/main`. Never edit `~/nexus-code` itself. It is the owner's checkout, and `/usr/local/bin/nexus` runs from it. |\n44-| Branch | `v1/plan-NN-pr-Nx`. One branch per slice. Never commit to `main` directly. |\n45-| HOME and state | Until PLAN-08 PR-8a lands, every test, build and probe run sets `HOME=$(mktemp -d)` and `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=\"$HOME/.config\"`. After PR-8a, the test setup enforces this, but scripts and probes outside `npm test` must still set it. **Never** run anything that writes to the real `~/.nexus` or `~/.config/nexus`. |\n46-| Test runs | Full suites run on a **snapshot copy** of the worktree (`git worktree add` at the commit under test, or `git archive`), never on the tree being edited. `tsx` reads `src/` live. |\n47-| Builds | Each worktree builds into its own `core/target`, and `cli/dist` is per worktree. To share the Rust build cache, set `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` per worktree, never to one shared directory. |\n48-| Ports | Mock servers bind `127.0.0.1:0` (an ephemeral port) and publish the chosen port to a file. Fixed ports are forbidden. |\n49-| Processes | Kill only PIDs you started, taken from `$!` or `ps -eo pid,args \\| grep -v grep`. **Never `pkill -f` or `killall`.** Long background jobs are stopped through the agent's own task controls. |\n50:| Full test suites | The PTY and provider tests are timing-sensitive. Take the claim `SUITE` before running a full suite (`make test`, or `npm test` over all files). While anyone holds `SUITE`, nobody runs other suites or targeted test files on the same machine. Concurrent load caused a false failure on 2026-10-15: `cloud_providers` saw an extra provider request after a load-induced timeout. Release the claim when the run ends. |\n51:| Inference | There is one inference host (DEC-16). Any run that uses the gateway takes the claim `GATEWAY` first. That includes eval runs, live checks and stripped-proxy runs. One gateway run at a time, which keeps eval timings valid and prevents overload. Small cloud checks (E5) take `CLOUD` to avoid duplicate spend. |\n52-| Credentials | Agents never read or copy `~/.config/nexus/credentials.json`, `config.env` values, or gateway tokens. The stripped proxy reads the token file itself. |\n53-\n54-## 4. Hot files: serialise, do not merge blindly\n55-\n56:These files are touched by several plans. An agent editing one must hold its `FILE_` claim for the **shortest** period: edit, test, push, merge, then release.\n57-\n58-| File | Plans that touch it | Rule |\n59-|---|---|---|\n60-| `cli/src/agent.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 04, 05, 11 | Claim required. Keep each change local to the line ranges the plan names. Rebase immediately before merge and re-run that slice's tests on the rebased snapshot. |\n61-| `cli/src/index.ts` | 01, 03, 04, 06, 08, 10, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-06 PR-6b (unified launcher) lands first.** Other plans add commands and flags afterwards. |\n62-| `cli/src/tools.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 06, 11 | Claim required. **PLAN-02 PR-2b (signal plumbing) lands before PLAN-03 PR-3c (`safeFetch`)**. |\n63-| `cli/src/sidecar.ts` | 01, 02, 03, 05, 08 | Claim required |\n64-| `cli/src/doctor.ts` | 01, 03, 05, 06, 08 | Claim required. Each plan adds **new check functions** in separate blocks. Only PLAN-06 PR-6d changes existing checks. |\n--\n71-| `core/src/runner.rs`, `core/src/main.rs` | 01, 02, 06 | Claim required. The protocol version number is owned by PLAN-02 PR-2a. PLAN-06 PR-6c only reads it. |\n72-| `core/src/bin/nexus_tui/{live_state,live_view}.rs` | 01, 02 | Claim required |\n73-| `package.json`, `cli/package.json`, `core/Cargo.toml`, lockfiles | 01, 06, 07, 08 | **Only one agent at a time.** Version fields are written only by `scripts/sync-version.mjs`. Dependency changes are separate commits that touch only the lockfile and manifest. |\n74-| `harness.sh`, `install.sh`, `scripts/package-linux.sh`, `.github/workflows/*` | 05, 06, 07, 09, C1 | Claim required. Workflow edits are separate commits. |\n75-| `cli/dist/**` | all, while tracked | Do not commit `cli/dist` from slices. PLAN-07 PR-7d untracks it (DEC-10). Until that merges, only the merging agent rebuilds and commits dist, in a final separate commit, after rebasing. |\n76:| `docs/USER_MANUAL.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `CLOUD_PROVIDERS.md`, `quickstart.md` | 05, 09 and doc tasks in every plan | Edit only the sections your slice changes. Hold a short `FILE_` claim. |\n77-\n78-### Ledger updates (`docs/v1.0.0/REQUIREMENTS.md`)\n79-\n80-A slice changes only the rows for its own requirement IDs: the status, test-reference and evidence cells, in place. It never reorders, adds or removes rows, and never edits another slice's rows. New requirements discovered during work go to §6, not into the ledger. Because rows are independent lines, git merges these edits cleanly. If a conflict appears anyway, resolve it row by row, keeping both changes.\n81-\n82-## 5. Commit and merge discipline\n83-\n84-1. Before **every** commit, compare `git rev-parse HEAD` with the commit you last made. Then run `git log <yours>..HEAD` on the branch. If anything foreign appears, stop and reconcile. Never commit generated artifacts (evidence, dist, lockfiles) over a branch that moved (lessons 2026-10-13).\n85-2. Commits are scoped to the slice. Do not reformat or clean up files outside the boundary.\n86:3. Merge order follows the precedence rules in §4 and the ROADMAP milestone order. Within a milestone, the slice with fewer hot-file claims merges first.\n87-4. Rebase onto `origin/main` immediately before merging. Re-run the slice's targeted tests, plus `make test`, on a snapshot of the rebased commit. Merge only if both pass.\n88:5. Pushing, tagging, publishing and changing GitHub state (issues, milestones, releases) are **owner-approved actions** (DECISIONS X-list). Agents prepare them and do not perform them.\n89-6. Preserve unrelated user work: the untracked `nexus-continuity-validation/`, the stashes, and any dirty files you did not create. Never stash, reset, clean or check out over them.\n90-\n91-## 6. Coordination records\n92-\n93-| Record | Where | Purpose |\n94-|---|---|---|\n95:| Claims | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/` | Who owns which slice or file right now |\n96:| Claim log | `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims.log` (append-only, one line per event) | Claim, release, broken claim, boundary request |\n97:| Boundary requests and discovered requirements | The PR description, under the heading `Needs from other slices`. Mirrored as one line in the claim log. | So the owning slice's agent can pick it up. Never fixed silently out of scope. |\n98-| Slice evidence | `docs/reviews/evidence/v1/<plan>-<slice>/` (new directory per slice) | No shared evidence files, so agents cannot overwrite each other's evidence |\n99-\n100-## 7. Safe parallel lanes\n101-\n102:The following groupings can run at the same time with no hot-file contention beyond §4 claims. They are listed by milestone.\n103-\n104-| Phase | Parallel lanes |\n105-|---|---|\n106-| M0 | (a) PLAN-08 PR-8a, which must merge first. Then in parallel: (b) PLAN-06 PR-6a, (c) PLAN-09 PR-9a. |\n107-| M1 | (a) PLAN-01 PR-1a → PR-1b → PR-1c (sequential, owns `capabilities.ts`, `agent.ts` dispatch). (b) PLAN-02 PR-2a (Rust only). (c) PLAN-03 PR-3a → PR-3b (config/egress). (d) PLAN-11 PR-11a (`parser.ts`) and PR-11b (`tester.ts`, after PR-1c). Lanes (b), (c) and (d) do not touch `capabilities.ts`. |\n108-| M2 (alongside M1) | (a) PLAN-07 PR-7a/7b/7c (workflows, scripts). (b) PLAN-06 PR-6b after PLAN-01 PR-1b (shares `index.ts` flags). |\n109-| M3 | (a) PLAN-04 (`agent.ts` turn, operation runner). (b) PLAN-05 (`client.ts`, provider modules). (c) PLAN-10 harness (`eval/`, no product files). (d) Held-out task authoring by the agent author, **outside the repository**: no access to Nexus source or the development set (DEC-05). |\n110:| M4 (alongside M3) | PLAN-08 PR-8b..8e. Coordinate with PLAN-04 on `state.ts` through `FILE_` claims. |\n111:| M5 | **Serial on `GATEWAY`.** The eval runner, the stripped-proxy runs and the independent reviewer's live task each take the `GATEWAY` claim. The terminal matrix (E3) and the install matrix (E2) can run in parallel with gateway runs. |\n112-\n113-## 8. Independence constraints\n114-\n115-- The held-out task author must not see Nexus source, the development tasks or tuning history. It runs in a separate session with only the task schema and external repositories. Its output is sealed by a sha256 manifest committed before RC1.\n116-- The independent reviewer in ACCEPTANCE §8 must not be the agent or session that implemented the slices under review.\n117-- An implementing agent never edits `eval/heldout-manifest.json`, scorer thresholds or ACCEPTANCE §6.4 after the freeze.\n# Nexus Code v1.0.0: release qualification procedure\n\nThis is the procedure that decides whether a release candidate (RC) may become v1.0.0. It defines:\n\n- the environments;\n- the workflow matrix;\n- the evidence each step must produce;\n- explicit pass/fail gates.\n\nThree separate questions are judged and recorded separately:\n\n| Question | Who answers it |\n|---|---|\n| **Implementation self-assessment** | Did the implementer's tests pass? |\n| **Product qualification** | Did the RC meet these gates on real workflows? |\n| **Independent acceptance** | Did a reviewer other than the implementer agree? |\n\nA gate passes only on archived evidence bound to the source commit or installed RC artifact that the gate evaluates. Source-level evidence names the exact source commit; installed-RC evidence names both that commit and the installed package digest. A green test suite alone never qualifies the product.\n\n## 1.1 Release-candidate lifecycle and evidence identity\n\nThe release sequence has five distinct stages. Passing an earlier stage is a prerequisite for the next stage, not a substitute for it. Every G0–G4 acceptance ID remains mandatory; the stage determines which exact input and evidence it evaluates, not whether the check applies.\n\n1. **Pre-RC source gates.** Before creating an RC tag, run required source CI and every G0–G4 check whose acceptance method evaluates source, a source-built test binary, or an environment without requiring a published RC. Record the full commit ID and check results. Source checks do not establish behavior of the installed package.\n2. **RC artifact construction.** After the required owner authorization, create the version-matched RC tag on that exact source commit and use the repository release workflow to build and publish the RC artifacts. Record the workflow run, tag commit, artifact digests, and provenance. The workflow-produced artifacts are the candidates for qualification; a local build or a different commit is not interchangeable. RC artifact publication is not product qualification or final release acceptance.\n3. **RC artifact gates.** Before installed-RC product qualification, run every G0–G4 check whose acceptance method requires the RC archive, RC attestation/release, or workflow-produced artifact. This includes ACC-SUP-01/02/03/06/08. ACC-PKG checks that explicitly require an installed package run against this RC as specified by their test method. Record the RC tag commit, workflow run, artifact digests, and each check result. A source-level analogue does not replace an artifact-bound check.\n4. **Installed-RC qualification.** Install the workflow-produced RC package using the documented procedure and run the applicable product, provider, task-quality, terminal, installation, and independent-acceptance gates in §§4–8. Bind each result to both the RC source commit and installed package digest. If a source change or rebuilt artifact changes either identity, rerun every affected gate against the new candidate and retain prior results as historical evidence.\n5. **Stable promotion and publication.** After exact-RC acceptance and G6 pass, create a stable-version commit directly on the accepted RC commit, changing only the synchronized version metadata required for v1.0.0. Record both commits and the version-only diff. Run required source CI and all applicable source G0–G4 checks on this exact stable-version commit before tagging. If any other source changes, restart RC construction and qualification. Then create the `v1.0.0` tag on that checked commit and build a draft release. Keep its assets unpublished while verifying the stable artifact identity, provenance, package contents, installation, and publication metadata. Compare the stable package contents with the accepted RC; if packaged behavior or content differs beyond version-specific metadata, rerun affected installed-RC qualification gates and obtain independent acceptance for the stable artifact. Publish the draft only after these checks pass and the signed acceptance disposition is recorded. Record the published digests and provenance.\n\nThe final G6 decision in §10 applies to promoting the accepted RC toward a stable release. It requires the applicable gates to pass for the accepted RC and authorizes the version-only stable commit and its source checks, followed by the stable tag and draft build; it does not waive source CI, artifact identity, installed-RC qualification, or any threshold. Public publication is a separate post-build gate in stage 5 above. The release workflow must keep stable assets in draft state until stable artifact checks pass. Source verification at integration 6a16cb6 confirms the tag-triggered release workflow now supports published RC prereleases and draft stable releases. The earlier immediate-stable-publication source blocker is resolved; an actual stable draft build, artifact verification, signed acceptance, and deliberate publication are still required and remain unproven until their run evidence is recorded.\n\nExact-RC requirements records identify at least one implementer and an independent reviewer. For a blocking requirement recorded as `tested` instead of `product-qualified`, the cited decision must be explicitly allowlisted for that requirement in the approved qualification-exceptions table below, and its `DECISIONS.md` section must contain a matching requirement-specific line of the form `Qualification applicability: REQ-... = NOT REQUIRED ...`. A record-supplied or unrelated decision cannot authorize an exception. The record must include digest-bound evidence for every `ACC-*` criterion in that requirement's ledger Acceptance column.\n\nThe post-RC closure requirements listed below may remain `open`, `partial`, or `implemented` in the source ledger at the RC commit because their remaining acceptance is completed during G6 or installed-RC qualification. The bare `--enforce` invocation is the pre-RC source gate: it checks every other B/RR row against the source threshold and reports mapped rows as deferred, not accepted. At G6, the exact-RC invocation must provide a record meeting the normal B/RR status threshold and digest-bound evidence for every criterion in this table. The table also supplies the criterion mapping for rows whose ledger points to an ACCEPTANCE section instead of naming ACC IDs. The source ledger is never rewritten by the record. Any below-tested required row omitted from this table still fails.\n\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-ONLY:START -->\n### Qualification-only requirements for exact-RC enforcement\n\n| Requirement | Acceptance criteria required in the exact-RC record | Remaining stage |\n|---|---|---|\n| REQ-REL-003 | ACC-PLAT | Installed-RC platform and Node-version matrix |\n| REQ-AGT-002 | ACC-VER-03, ACC-VER-04, ACC-VER-09, ACC-QUAL-T2, ACC-QUAL-T3, ACC-QUAL-T4 | Installed-RC T2/T3/T4 outcome thresholds |\n| REQ-AGT-010 | ACC-TRUST-04 | Independent acceptance of the DEC-04 v1 subset |\n| REQ-PRV-003 | ACC-INF-10 | Installed-RC standalone and absent-gateway behavior |\n| REQ-REL-005 | ACC-SUP-04, ACC-SUP-05 | Exact-RC security and dependency artifact checks |\n| REQ-AGT-110 | ACC-CONT-10, ACC-GOV-06 | Installed-RC continuity and independent acceptance |\n| REQ-PRV-111 | ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC provider verification |\n| REQ-REL-114 | ACC-SUP-08 | Exact-RC cross-runner reproducibility |\n| REQ-QUAL-101 | ACC-QUAL-T2 | Installed-RC held-out T2 qualification |\n| REQ-QUAL-107 | ACC-PRV-STANDALONE, ACC-PRV-LIVE | Installed-RC standalone and provider matrix |\n| REQ-QUAL-108 | ACC-TUI-STEER, ACC-TUI-A11Y | Installed-RC automated and scripted PTY acceptance; the manual E3 matrix is waived by DEC-20 |\n| REQ-QUAL-109 | ACC-QUAL-T3 | Installed-RC T3 long-task qualification |\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-ONLY:END -->\n\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-EXCEPTIONS:START -->\n### Approved exact-RC qualification exceptions\n\n| Requirement | Approved decision | Scope |\n|---|---|---|\n<!-- RC-QUALIFICATION-EXCEPTIONS:END -->\n\n## 1. Rules\n\n1. The RC is built and published by the release workflow from a clean tag. Its version is identical across all components (REQ-REL-103).\n2. All qualification runs use the **installed RC package**, installed with the documented one-line command. They never use a checkout build. The only exception is CI unit and PTY suites, which run from source at the same commit.\n3. Thresholds (DEC-05) are frozen before the first M5 run. A failing gate is fixed in code and the run is repeated as `rc.N+1`. A threshold is never relaxed after results are known.\n4. Evidence goes to `docs/reviews/evidence/v1-qualification/<rc>/`. The directory contains:\n   - `manifest.json`: commit, package sha256, environment, model fingerprint, dates;\n   - raw outputs;\n   - a `SHA256SUMS` file.\n\n   Secrets are redacted before commit.\n5. Any step that cannot run in the available environment is recorded as **NOT RUN** with its reason. A NOT RUN on a blocker gate fails the gate.\n\n## 2. Environments\n\n| ID | Environment | Used for |\n|---|---|---|\n| E1 | GitHub Actions: ubuntu-22.04 and ubuntu-24.04; Node 22 and 24; pinned Rust | Unit, integration and PTY suites; packaging; reproducibility |\n| E2 | Docker clean images: ubuntu:22.04, ubuntu:24.04, debian:12, fedora:40. Each with no Node, then with Node 20, Node 22 and Node 24. Non-root user. | Installer, upgrade, rollback, uninstall, platform failure messages |\n| E3 | Operator dev box: Linux x86-64, real terminals (xterm, kitty, tmux, VS Code terminal, GNOME Terminal) | Terminal matrix, live sessions |\n| E4 | Homelab-ai gateway, the **only** inference path (DEC-16): direct, and through `eval/standalone/plain_proxy.mjs` | Task-quality runs, standalone qualification, T3/T4 |\n| E5 | Cloud, small spend: ChatGPT sign-in (experimental tier) and OpenRouter with a pinned model; OpenAI and Anthropic keys if added | Provider verify; one live task per supported cloud provider |\n\n## 3. Gate checklist (G0–G4: automated acceptance IDs)\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":31033}},"started_at_ms":1793354532198,"completed_at_ms":1793354532199}}
